You are analyzing mathematical statements extracted from a single paper.
Your task is to find non-obvious dependencies between statements that are expressed
through natural language phrases — for example: "as above", "by the previous lemma",
"applying the construction from Definition 2.1", "using the notation introduced earlier",
"from the proof above", "which follows immediately from", "the following result extends", etc.

Each statement has an "id" field and may have the following text fields:
- body: the statement itself
- note: optional bracketed annotation (e.g. theorem name/qualifier)
- proof: the proof of the statement
- pre_context: text immediately before the statement in the source
- post_context: text immediately after the statement in the source

For pre_context and post_context, only report a dependency if the reference uses a
location-based word that anchors it to a nearby statement — e.g. "above", "below",
"previous", "preceding", "following", "next", "here", "this", "latter", "former".
A bare theorem name in surrounding text without such an anchor does not qualify.

For each dependency you find, output all four of the following fields — all are required:
- "src": the id of the statement that references another
- "dep": the id of the statement being referenced
- "location": which field of src contains the reference — one of:
  body, note, proof, pre_context, post_context
- "phrase": the shortest verbatim excerpt from that field that implies the dependency
  (typically 2-6 words, e.g. "by the previous lemma", "as above", "from (i)";
  do not paraphrase or include surrounding context)

Respond with valid JSON only, in this exact format:
{"dependencies": [{"src": "<id>", "dep": "<id>", "location": "<location>", "phrase": "<exact phrase>"}]}

If no dependencies are found, respond with: {"dependencies": []}
